    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>From the 1st millennium BCE to the Spanish conquest, the Maya of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a> built hundreds of cities, from El Mirador and Tikal to Chichén Itzá, but never a single state.</li><li>Their scribes created the most complete writing system of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, recording the deeds of holy kings in precisely dated inscriptions, most of which have been deciphered since the 1950s.</li><li>The southern lowland cities were abandoned in the 9th and 10th centuries, but Maya kingdoms lasted in Yucatán and the highlands until the Spanish conquest, and well over six million Maya live today.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 2018 an airborne laser survey of the forests of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> revealed more than 60,000 ancient Maya structures, from houses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> to causeways and defensive walls, many of them previously unknown.</p></aside>
    </section>

